  "actual_observations": [
    "The original scan and modern complete preview show one coordinate grid, a common vertex O, two upward-opening and two downward-opening parabolas. The complete modern square, rightmost branches, bottommost branches, axis arrowheads, and numeric ticks are visible.",
    "Contrary to a possible unlabelled-curve premise, the actual images visibly label the curves with Roman numerals I, II, III and IV. No invented A/B curve identifiers are needed.",
    "I is the wider upward-opening curve; II is the narrower upward-opening curve. III is the wider downward-opening curve; IV is the narrower downward-opening curve. All four curves are solid.",
    "From the coordinate grid, I passes through (1, 1), II through (1, 2), III through (2, -1), and IV through (1, -1). The plotted ordinate is not itself the coefficient when the abscissa is 2."
  ],
  "independent_source_solution": {
    "method": "For a visible non-vertex point (x, y) on each curve, x is nonzero and y = a x^2 implies a = y / x^2. The common vertex (0, 0) cannot determine a because division by x^2 would divide by zero.",
